1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,125
Meet Vera Rubin.

2
00:00:02,075 --> 00:00:05,600
This is episode eight of From Sand to GPU.

3
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:11,287
Vera Rubin is NVIDIA’s rack-scale AI platform succeeding Grace Blackwell-era systems.

4
00:00:11,287 --> 00:00:16,537
It co-designs multiple chips so the rack — and the POD — is the unit of compute.

5
00:00:16,537 --> 00:00:22,862
That framing is confirmed from NVIDIA Newsroom and the NVIDIA Technical Blog in early twenty-twenty-six.

6
00:00:22,862 --> 00:00:24,800
Naming first.

7
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:31,250
Vera is the custom Arm-compatible CPU with Olympus cores, on a successor trajectory to Grace.

8
00:00:31,250 --> 00:00:36,387
Rubin is the GPU accelerator with HBM4 and Transformer Engine.

9
00:00:36,387 --> 00:00:42,412
Vera Rubin platform means the full chip, rack, networking, storage, and software stack.

10
00:00:42,412 --> 00:00:49,512
NVL72 is the flagship rack integrating seventy-two Rubin GPUs and thirty-six Vera CPUs.

11
00:00:49,512 --> 00:01:00,725
NVIDIA states the platform brings together Vera CPU, Rubin GPU, NVLink 6 Switch, ConnectX-9 SuperNIC, BlueField-4 DPU, and Spectrum-6 Ethernet switch.

12
00:01:00,725 --> 00:01:07,125
A GTC twenty-twenty-six update newly integrates NVIDIA Groq 3 LPU as a seventh chip.

13
00:01:07,125 --> 00:01:13,275
The Technical Blog initially framed six new chips; the Newsroom update adds Groq 3 — confirmed.

14
00:01:13,275 --> 00:01:36,887
Public rack SKUs include Vera Rubin NVL72; a Vera CPU Rack with two-hundred-fifty-six Vera CPUs in a liquid-cooled MGX form; a Groq 3 LPX Rack with two-hundred-fifty-six LPU processors; a BlueField-4 STX Storage Rack for AI-native KV-cache and context memory; and a Spectrum-6 SPX Ethernet Rack for scale-out, configurable for Spectrum-X or Quantum-X800 InfiniBand.

15
00:01:36,887 --> 00:01:41,712
Partner products start in the second half of twenty-twenty-six — confirmed.

16
00:01:41,712 --> 00:01:49,037
Named clouds include AWS, Google Cloud, Azure, and OCI, plus several GPU cloud providers.

17
00:01:49,037 --> 00:02:13,587
Selected confirmed figures from NVIDIA tables: Rubin carries three-hundred-thirty-six billion transistors on the full chip, versus two-hundred-eight billion cited for Blackwell; two compute dies; fifty PFLOPS NVFP4 inference and thirty-five PFLOPS NVFP4 training; up to two-hundred-eighty-eight gigabytes of HBM4 at twenty-two terabytes per second per GPU; and three-point-six terabytes per second bidirectional NVLink 6 per GPU.

18
00:02:13,587 --> 00:02:26,962
Vera offers eighty-eight Olympus cores and one-hundred-seventy-six spatial multithreads, up to one-point-five terabytes of LPDDR5X at up to one-point-two terabytes per second, and one-point-eight terabytes per second NVLink-C2C.

19
00:02:26,962 --> 00:02:32,137
NVL72 is seventy-two GPUs plus thirty-six CPUs.

20
00:02:32,137 --> 00:02:42,212
Honesty box for trackers: platform chip list, NVL72 composition, HBM4, and NVLink 6 bandwidths are confirmed from NVIDIA primary sources.

21
00:02:42,212 --> 00:02:55,187
TSMC process node for Rubin — for example N3 or N3P — is likely in industry press but not consistently printed on the NVIDIA newsroom pages reviewed; treat node as non-confirmed until NVIDIA or TSMC primary.

22
00:02:55,187 --> 00:03:02,775
CoWoS-L as packaging vehicle is likely from press and prior Blackwell path, but unknown as a NVIDIA product-page one-liner.

23
00:03:02,775 --> 00:03:08,125
Exact HBM stack count, often discussed as eight, is likely secondary.

24
00:03:08,125 --> 00:03:14,162
Die size in square millimeters and unit BOM dollar cost are unknown publicly in the sources reviewed.

25
00:03:14,162 --> 00:03:19,512
That is episode eight: Meet Vera Rubin, grounded in confirmed NVIDIA figures.

26
00:03:19,512 --> 00:03:28,087
Next time in the series arc: Vera Rubin architecture and the TSMC path — a future episode on how the platform maps onto process and packaging.

